\title{Pair-Star Constructions for Berge-Triangle-Free\\
$3$-Uniform Hypergraphs with Bounded Matching Number}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 16, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a basic extremal construction for $3$-uniform hypergraphs that simultaneously excludes Berge triangles and prescribes the matching number. Given integers $s,t\geq0$, take $s$ disjoint vertex pairs and $t$ outside vertices, and form one hyperedge from each pair together with each outside vertex. We prove directly that this pair-star hypergraph has $2s+t$ vertices and $st$ edges, is $3$-uniform and Berge-triangle-free, and has matching number $\min(s,t)$. We also determine its complete edge-intersection pattern. Substituting $t=n-2s$ yields, for every $n\geq3s$, an $n$-vertex Berge-triangle-free $3$-uniform hypergraph with matching number exactly $s$ and $s(n-2s)$ edges. Thus the construction establishes the lower-bound side of the anticipated extremal formula. We give construction, recognition, and matching-witness algorithms, discuss applications, and delineate the structural upper-bound and uniqueness questions that remain.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A hypergraph generalizes a graph by allowing an edge to contain more than two vertices. An $r$-uniform hypergraph is a pair $\mathcal H=(V,E)$ in which $V$ is finite and every member of $E$ is an $r$-element subset of $V$. Uniform hypergraphs model interactions of fixed arity: triples in a $3$-uniform hypergraph can represent three-way collaborations, resource bundles, database transactions, or combinatorial blocks.

To transfer graph patterns into hypergraphs, the Berge notion asks that distinct graph edges be represented by distinct hyperedges containing them. For a triangle this means three core vertices and three distinct hyperedges, each representing one core pair. A second statistic is the matching number $\nu(\mathcal H)$, the greatest number of pairwise disjoint hyperedges. Bounding it limits how many interactions can occur independently.

The construction developed here is based on $s$ disjoint two-vertex spines. Every edge consists of a complete spine and one vertex from a common outside set. This overlap pattern serves two purposes. All edges associated with one spine intersect, so a matching selects at most one of them. Meanwhile, the rigid edge type prevents three distinct hyperedges from representing the three sides of a triangle.

Our principal theorem is constructive. For every $n\geq3s$, it supplies an $n$-vertex example with exactly $s(n-2s)$ edges and matching number $s$. Consequently, any universal extremal upper bound in this range must be at least $s(n-2s)$. This does not itself establish that all admissible hypergraphs have at most this many edges, nor uniqueness at equality; those require separate structural arguments.

\section{Definitions and extremal framework}

\begin{definition}[Uniform hypergraph]
A finite hypergraph is a pair $\mathcal H=(V,E)$, where $V$ is a finite set and $E$ is a set of subsets of $V$. It is \emph{$r$-uniform} if $|e|=r$ for every $e\in E$. A $3$-uniform hypergraph is also called a $3$-graph.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Matching]
A subfamily $M\subseteq E$ is a \emph{matching} if $e\cap f=\varnothing$ for all distinct $e,f\in M$. Its maximum possible cardinality is the \emph{matching number}
\[
\nu(\mathcal H)=\max\{|M|:M\subseteq E\text{ is a matching}\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Berge triangle]
A Berge triangle consists of distinct core vertices $a,b,c\in V$ and distinct representing hyperedges $e_{ab},e_{bc},e_{ca}\in E$ such that
\[
\{a,b\}\subseteq e_{ab},\qquad
\{b,c\}\subseteq e_{bc},\qquad
\{c,a\}\subseteq e_{ca}.
\]
A hypergraph is \emph{Berge-triangle-free} if it contains no such configuration.
\end{definition}

Distinctness of representatives is essential: one $3$-edge contains all three pairs among its vertices but does not alone form a Berge triangle.

For fixed $n$ and $s$, the bounded-matching extremal problem asks for the maximum number of edges among all $n$-vertex Berge-triangle-free $3$-graphs $\mathcal H$ satisfying $\nu(\mathcal H)\leq s$. A construction with $m$ edges gives a lower bound of $m$; a universal comparison against every admissible hypergraph is needed for a matching upper bound.

\section{The pair-star construction}

Let $s,t\geq0$. Choose pairwise distinct vertices
\[
u_1,v_1,u_2,v_2,\ldots,u_s,v_s
\]
and a disjoint outside set $X=\{x_1,\ldots,x_t\}$. For $1\leq i\leq s$, set $P_i=\{u_i,v_i\}$. The $P_i$ are pairwise disjoint. Define
\[
V_{s,t}=\bigcup_{i=1}^{s}P_i\;\dot\cup\;X,
\qquad
E_{s,t}=\{P_i\cup\{x\}:1\leq i\leq s,\ x\in X\}.
\]
Write $\mathcal P_{s,t}=(V_{s,t},E_{s,t})$ and $e(i,x)=P_i\cup\{x\}$. For fixed $i$, the edges $e(i,x)$ form a pair-star with spine $P_i$.

\begin{lemma}[Injective edge parametrization]
If $e(i,x)=e(j,y)$, then $i=j$ and $x=y$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each edge contains exactly two spine vertices and one outside vertex. Equality forces equality of the unique outside vertices, hence $x=y$. The remaining two-element sets are $P_i$ and $P_j$; their equality forces $i=j$ because the spines are disjoint and separately indexed.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniformity and exact counts]
For all $s,t\geq0$, $\mathcal P_{s,t}$ is $3$-uniform, has exactly $2s+t$ vertices, and exactly $st$ edges.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every edge is the disjoint union of a two-element spine and one outside vertex. The vertex set is the disjoint union of $s$ two-element sets and a $t$-element set, so $|V_{s,t}|=2s+t$. There are $st$ parameter pairs $(i,x)$, and the preceding lemma shows that they produce distinct edges. Thus $|E_{s,t}|=st$. The same calculation covers $s=0$ or $t=0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact intersection geometry}

\begin{theorem}[Edge-intersection formula]
For all valid $i,j,x,y$,
\[
|e(i,x)\cap e(j,y)|=
\begin{cases}
3,&i=j\text{ and }x=y,\\
2,&i=j\text{ and }x\ne y,\\
1,&i\ne j\text{ and }x=y,\\
0,&i\ne j\text{ and }x\ne y.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $i=j$, both edges contain $P_i$. They are equal when $x=y$, and otherwise share exactly the two spine vertices. If $i\ne j$, disjointness of $P_i$ and $P_j$ eliminates every spine intersection. The edges then meet precisely when their outside vertices agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Edges from different pair-stars intersect in at most one vertex.
\end{corollary}

This formula is a recognition signature. Within a star, two distinct edges intersect in two vertices; edges in different stars never intersect in more than one. It will also control matchings.

\section{Matching number}

\begin{theorem}[Matching upper bound]
Every matching $M\subseteq E_{s,t}$ satisfies $|M|\leq s$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Assign to each $e(i,x)\in M$ its index $i$. Two distinct matching edges cannot have the same index, because then they share $P_i$. Thus the index map is injective on $M$, while its codomain has $s$ elements.
\end{proof}

A second restriction is just as immediate: disjoint edges must use distinct outside vertices, so every matching also has size at most $t$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact matching number]
For all $s,t\geq0$,
\[
\nu(\mathcal P_{s,t})=\min(s,t).
\]
In particular, if $s\leq t$, then $\nu(\mathcal P_{s,t})=s$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The spine-index argument gives $\nu\leq s$, and injectivity of the outside-vertex assignment on disjoint edges gives $\nu\leq t$. Hence $\nu\leq\min(s,t)$. Let $m=\min(s,t)$. Choose $m$ distinct spines and $m$ distinct outside vertices, pair them bijectively, and select the corresponding edges. Distinct selected edges have different spines and different outside vertices, so the intersection formula makes them disjoint. Thus $\nu\geq m$.
\end{proof}

The formula displays two independent bottlenecks: every member of a disjoint edge family consumes one spine and one outside vertex.

\section{Exclusion of Berge triangles}

\begin{theorem}[Berge-triangle freeness]
For every $s,t\geq0$, $\mathcal P_{s,t}$ contains no Berge triangle.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Call a vertex spinal if it belongs to some $P_i$ and outside if it belongs to $X$. Every edge contains exactly two spinal vertices forming one complete spine and exactly one outside vertex.

Suppose distinct $a,b,c$ and distinct $e_{ab},e_{bc},e_{ca}$ form a Berge triangle. Two outside vertices cannot form a represented pair, since no edge contains two outside vertices. Thus at most one core vertex is outside.

If all three are spinal, every represented pair must lie in one spine, because no edge contains spinal vertices from different spines. Since $a,b$ lie in one spine and $b,c$ lie in one spine, disjointness of the spine partition forces these spines to coincide. Then three distinct vertices would lie in a two-element spine, a contradiction.

Suppose instead exactly one core vertex is outside, say $c$, while $a,b$ are spinal. Since an edge contains $a$ and $b$, they must form a complete spine $P_i$. The unique edge containing both $a$ and $c$ is $P_i\cup\{c\}$; the unique edge containing both $b$ and $c$ is the same set. Hence $e_{ca}=e_{bc}$, contradicting distinctness of representatives. Every case is impossible.
\end{proof}

The argument exposes the obstruction: outside--outside pairs and cross-spine spinal pairs cannot occur, while a spine linked to one outside vertex has only one representative available for both incident sides.

\section{Specialization to a prescribed vertex count}

\begin{theorem}[Extremal lower-bound construction]
Let $n,s\geq0$ satisfy $3s\leq n$. There exists an $n$-vertex, $3$-uniform, Berge-triangle-free hypergraph $\mathcal H$ such that
\[
\nu(\mathcal H)=s
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
|E(\mathcal H)|=s(n-2s).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $t=n-2s$. The hypothesis implies $s\leq t$. For $\mathcal H=\mathcal P_{s,t}$, the vertex count is
\[
2s+t=2s+(n-2s)=n,
\]
and the edge count is $st=s(n-2s)$. The exact matching theorem gives $\nu(\mathcal H)=s$, and the preceding theorem gives Berge-triangle freeness.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Extremal lower bound]
For $n\geq3s$, the maximum number of edges in an $n$-vertex Berge-triangle-free $3$-graph with matching number at most $s$ is at least $s(n-2s)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The preceding construction is admissible and has that many edges.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Scope]
The theorem is a construction result. It does not prove that every admissible hypergraph satisfies $|E(\mathcal H)|\leq s(n-2s)$, nor that every equality case is isomorphic to $\mathcal P_{s,n-2s}$. Those conclusions require a universal upper-bound proof and a separate equality analysis.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Edge generation}
Represent spine vertices by $(i,0)$ and $(i,1)$ and outside vertices by $x_j$. For all $s t$ pairs $(i,j)$, output $\{(i,0),(i,1),x_j\}$. The running time and output storage are $\Theta(st)$, optimal because every edge must be emitted.

\subsection{Maximum matching witness}
Let $m=\min(s,t)$. For $0\leq i<m$, select the edge joining spine $i$ to outside vertex $x_i$. The edges have distinct spines and outside vertices, so they are disjoint. The algorithm uses $\Theta(m)$ time and outputs a maximum matching.

\subsection{Exhaustive triangle search}
For small inputs one may enumerate triples of core vertices and triples of distinct edges, accepting when each core pair lies in its assigned edge. A literal search takes $O(|V|^3|E|^3)$ time. Indexing each vertex pair by the containing edges improves practical performance. The structural theorem makes this unnecessary for pair-stars, but exhaustive search is useful for demonstrations and independent small-instance exploration.

\subsection{Intersection-profile verification}
For every pair of generated edges, compute the intersection size and compare it to the four-case formula. Since each edge has constant size, this requires $O((st)^2)$ pair checks. The resulting matrix has diagonal entries $3$, within-star off-diagonal entries $2$, same-outside cross-star entries $1$, and zeros elsewhere.

\section{Examples}

\begin{example}[Three stars on twelve vertices]
Take $s=3$ and $n=12$, whence $t=6$. The construction has six spine vertices and six outside vertices, and
\[
|E|=3\cdot6=18.
\]
Choosing three different spines and three different outside vertices produces a matching of size three. No matching of size four exists, and there is no Berge triangle.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Boundary case]
If $n=3s$, then $t=s$ and $|E|=s^2$. A maximum matching pairs every spine with a different outside vertex and uses every vertex exactly once, so it is a perfect matching.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Too few outside vertices]
For $s=5$ and $t=2$, the construction has ten edges and is Berge-triangle-free, but its matching number is two. This shows why $t\geq s$, equivalently $n\geq3s$ after specialization, is needed to realize matching number $s$.
\end{example}

\section{Applications and interpretation}

The construction exemplifies density through controlled overlap. A large edge family repeatedly uses fixed two-vertex resources. Repetition suppresses matchings because edges sharing a spine are incompatible, while the edge syntax is too rigid to support three distinct triangle representatives.

In scheduling, each spine can represent a two-resource package that every job in one class must reserve, while outside vertices are third resources. At most one job from each class can occur in a disjoint schedule. In transactional systems, each pair-star represents operations sharing two locks; many operations can be specified, but few have disjoint lock sets. In experimental design, a fixed pair of mandatory conditions can be combined with many third factors without creating the incidence pattern of a Berge triangle.

The intersection formula also suggests clustering and recognition methods. Edges intersecting in two vertices should belong to one pair-star, while intersections of size one identify reused outside vertices. A noisy version may be recoverable from its edge-intersection graph. This motivates stability questions: if an admissible hypergraph has nearly $s(n-2s)$ edges, must it admit an approximate spine decomposition?

\section{Discussion and limitations}

The exact conclusions are: every construction edge has size three; the vertex and edge counts are $2s+t$ and $st$; the complete intersection profile is explicit; the matching number is $\min(s,t)$; no Berge triangle occurs; and the $n$-vertex specialization for $n\geq3s$ has matching number $s$ and $s(n-2s)$ edges.

A full extremal theorem must start with an arbitrary Berge-triangle-free $3$-graph $\mathcal H$ with $\nu(\mathcal H)\leq s$ and derive $|E(\mathcal H)|\leq s(n-2s)$. One natural route fixes a maximum matching, partitions vertices into matched and unmatched sets, studies how edges meet matching blocks, and sums local inequalities forced by Berge-triangle freeness. For uniqueness, equality in every local inequality must force $s$ disjoint spines and a common outside set with every possible pair-star edge present.

The present construction also does not settle analogous $4$-uniform formulas or exceptional congruence cases. These require different architectures. Nor does it derive an unrestricted result by optimizing over matching number.

\section{Future work}

The first priority is the universal upper bound on arbitrary finite vertex sets. Links and pair-codegrees should encode local behavior around a fixed maximum matching. The second is equality analysis supported by a label-independent notion of hypergraph isomorphism and transport of uniformity, Berge-freeness, cardinality, and matching number.

Further directions include $4$-uniform constructions, the exceptional case with matching number one and special vertex congruence, and finite enumeration of very small hypergraphs to investigate extremal counts and equality cases. Finally, a quantitative stability theorem should measure how many edge edits transform a nearly extremal admissible hypergraph into a pair-star.

\section{Conclusion}

The pair-star hypergraph turns overlap into an extremal resource. Its $s$ disjoint spine pairs each support $t$ edges, giving exactly $st$ triples on $2s+t$ vertices. The overlap that creates abundance prevents large matchings, while the one-spine-plus-one-outside format excludes Berge triangles. When $n\geq3s$, setting $t=n-2s$ yields an $n$-vertex construction with matching number exactly $s$ and $s(n-2s)$ edges.

This provides an exact lower bound for the bounded-matching Berge-triangle problem and a transparent blueprint for algorithms, recognition, and stability analysis. The next structural challenge is to prove that no admissible $3$-graph can do better and that equality forces this architecture.

\end{document}

